Under the California Voting Rights Act, cities switched from at-large to
district elections for their councils. A switch can only help Latino
candidates where Latinos live close enough together to form a majority in a
district. This vignette measures that concentration for five cities with
2010 census blocks.

The measures use the voting-age population (VAP). Courts look at citizen
voting-age population, which the Census Bureau does not publish for blocks,
so VAP is the closest block-level count.

```{r packages, message = FALSE, warning = FALSE}
library(placeindex)
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
options(tigris_use_cache = TRUE)
```

## Blocks

The package dataset `ca_place_concentration` lists every California place,
so it gives the five cities' place codes.

```{r places}
cities <- paste(c("Palmdale", "West Covina", "Victorville", "Anaheim",
                  "Modesto"), "city, California")
places <- ca_place_concentration |>
  filter(year == 2010, universe == "vap", place_name %in% cities) |>
  transmute(place_geoid, city = sub(" city, California", "", place_name))
places
```

`seg_blocks()` downloads block counts and polygons for the four counties the
cities lie in and assigns each block to its place.

```{r blocks, message = FALSE}
blocks <- seg_blocks(
  "CA", 2010, universe = "vap",
  county = c("Los Angeles", "San Bernardino", "Orange", "Stanislaus")
) |>
  filter(place_geoid %in% places$place_geoid)
nrow(blocks)
```

## How concentrated are Latinos?

`seg_measures()` gives the Latino share, two standard segregation measures
(dissimilarity and isolation) and the share of Latinos who live in
majority-Latino blocks for each city. It also returns exposure, which for
two groups is 1 minus isolation, so the table leaves it out.

```{r measures}
measures <- seg_measures(blocks, by = place_geoid, group = latino,
                         total = total)
measures |>
  left_join(places, by = "place_geoid", relationship = "one-to-one") |>
  select(city, pop_total, group_share, dissimilarity, isolation,
         majority_share)
```

`seg_clusters()` finds contiguous clusters of majority-Latino blocks and
compares the largest with an ideal district for a five-district council.
`largest_ratio` above 1 means the largest cluster alone could hold a
majority-Latino district. `n_districts_floor` adds up the whole districts
every cluster could hold; it is a rough guide, not a bound.

```{r clusters}
clusters <- seg_clusters(blocks, by = place_geoid, group = latino,
                         total = total, n_districts = 5)
clusters |>
  left_join(places, by = "place_geoid", relationship = "one-to-one") |>
  select(city, n_clusters, largest_pop, largest_share, largest_ratio,
         n_viable, n_districts_floor)
```

By default, blocks with no residents can join two clusters, because a
district can include them at no cost in population. Leaving them out is a
robustness check:

```{r no-bridge}
seg_clusters(blocks, by = place_geoid, group = latino, total = total,
             n_districts = 5, bridge_empty = FALSE) |>
  left_join(places, by = "place_geoid", relationship = "one-to-one") |>
  select(city, largest_ratio, n_viable, n_districts_floor)
```

## A concentration index

To rank the cities against the rest of the state, the next step uses
`ca_place_concentration`, which holds the same measures for every place.
It keeps places with at least 20,000 adults in 2010 and combines three
indicators that rise as Latinos become easier to place in majority
districts: isolation, the share in majority-Latino blocks, and the largest
cluster relative to an ideal district.

```{r index}
indicators <- c("isolation", "majority_share", "largest_ratio")
large_places <- ca_place_concentration |>
  filter(year == 2010, universe == "vap", pop_total >= 20000)
nrow(large_places)

index <- large_places |>
  idx_normalize(all_of(indicators), method = "rank") |>
  idx_combine(all_of(indicators), name = "concentration") |>
  mutate(rank = rank(-concentration, ties.method = "min"))
index |>
  inner_join(places, by = "place_geoid", relationship = "one-to-one") |>
  select(city, all_of(indicators), concentration, rank)
```

The ranks depend on choices: how to normalize, how to weight, and whether
to average arithmetically or geometrically. `idx_sensitivity()` redraws
those choices many times and reports each place's median rank with a 90%
interval.

```{r sensitivity}
sensitivity <- idx_sensitivity(
  large_places,
  cols = all_of(indicators),
  id = place_geoid,
  n_sims = 1000,
  seed = 2010
)
sensitivity |>
  inner_join(places, by = "place_geoid", relationship = "one-to-one") |>
  select(city, median_rank, rank_lower, rank_upper)
```

## Map: Palmdale

`seg_cluster_blocks()` labels each block with its cluster, which shows where
the largest cluster lies.

```{r map, fig.alt = "Map of Palmdale census blocks in 2010 in four classes: the largest majority-Latino cluster, the empty blocks that join it, other majority-Latino blocks, and other blocks.", fig.cap = ""}
palmdale_id <- places$place_geoid[places$city == "Palmdale"]
palmdale <- blocks |>
  filter(place_geoid == palmdale_id) |>
  seg_cluster_blocks(by = place_geoid, group = latino, total = total) |>
  mutate(type = case_when(
    cluster %in% 1 & total > 0 ~ "Largest majority-Latino cluster",
    cluster %in% 1 ~ "Empty blocks joining it",
    total > 0 & latino > 0.5 * total ~ "Other majority-Latino blocks",
    .default = "Other blocks"
  ))
ratio <- clusters$largest_ratio[clusters$place_geoid == palmdale_id]

ggplot(palmdale) +
  geom_sf(aes(fill = type), color = NA) +
  scale_fill_manual(
    values = c("Largest majority-Latino cluster" = "#1f5f99",
               "Empty blocks joining it" = "#a9c6e2",
               "Other majority-Latino blocks" = "#e39b2d",
               "Other blocks" = "#e6e6e6"),
    breaks = c("Largest majority-Latino cluster", "Empty blocks joining it",
               "Other majority-Latino blocks", "Other blocks"),
    name = NULL
  ) +
  labs(
    title = sprintf(
      "Palmdale's largest majority-Latino cluster is %.1f times an ideal district",
      ratio
    ),
    caption = paste("Census blocks, 2010, voting-age population.",
                    "Ideal district: the city's adults / 5.")
  ) +
  theme_void() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", plot.title.position = "plot")
```
